{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Who has counted the dust of Jacob, etc.</b> Now since the wicked one was cunning in his mind and seeking some means to harm Israel, as they said that he searched among them and among their forefathers to enter through that avenue, he also sought another branch that is uniquely suited to the kelipah, that it might hold sway over them and make an impression — and that is the matter of counting, as they said, of blessed memory (Bava Metzia 42a): blessing does not rest neither upon a thing that is counted, etc. And the wicked one wanted to introduce an evil eye through the count but did not find it, and he said: ״מִי מָנָה עֲפַר״ — for they were likened to him, as it is written (Genesis 13:16): ״כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ״. And this blessing, even though it was said to Abraham, was not fulfilled except in Jacob; therefore he said ״עֲפַר יַעֲקֹב״." "<b>Further,</b> he intended to say that no person had managed to count them — even when they wished to count themselves, HaShem commanded not to take a census except by means of something else, with weights or with half-shekels." "And his saying <b>and the number of the fourth part of Israel</b> — according to what we have prefaced, that he would speak in two levels: the multitudes of the people, who would be designated by the name Jacob, and the righteous, who would be designated by the name Israel. Therefore he said, concerning the multitudes, <b>Who has counted the dust of Jacob</b>, and concerning the righteous he said <b>and the number of the fourth part of Israel</b> — meaning that there are among them righteous ones beyond number. And know that the righteous, even when they are few, are extraordinary in their count, and the wicked are the reverse — even when they are many, their count is small, as they said, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 26a), that HaShem said to Hezekiah when he said before Him ״רַבִּים אֲשֶׁר אֶת שֶׁבְנָא״ etc., He said to him: ״the wicked have no count.״ And he was precise in saying ״רֹבַע״ because the righteous are settled (רְבוּצִים) in their tents, as it is written of Issachar (Genesis 49:14): ״רֹבֵץ בֵּין הַמִּשְׁפְּתָיִם״, and also because the Shekhinah rests (רוֹבַעַת) upon them, and also there is a sod in the matter, for they bring about the unification and coupling of the worlds." "<b>Further,</b> he alluded to what HaShem did miraculously for them in Egypt, when they decreed ״כָּל הַבֵּן הַיִּלּוֹד״ etc. — that they would count the pregnant women from the first night when the Jewish woman immersed, but it was of no avail, for HaShem performed wonders for them beyond number, as the matter is scattered in their sayings, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 23; Sotah 11a), and they could not determine their quarter-births — and that is what he said: ״וּמִסְפָּר אֶת רֹבַע יִשְׂרָאֵל״." "<b>Let my soul die the death of the upright.</b> These are his own words and not words of the holy spirit, and they have already said, of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni 768), that when he wished to speak words that would cause no harm to Israel, HaShem would let him speak as he pleased. And he said <b>the death of the upright</b> and did not say ״their death,״ which would refer back to Israel — the wicked one went to the extreme to request a great matter, as they said, of blessed memory, men of truth (Zohar vol. 1, 122b), that there are two levels among the righteous at the time of their death: one, that the soul leaves the body and goes without any barrier separating, without any impediment, until the place of supreme bliss; and there are righteous ones who at the departure of the soul do not attain this level, and are held back from going immediately and are delayed for some time if they have certain sins or the like — and even the slightest sin will cause this. Therefore Balaam chose to request the supreme level, that his soul at the time of death would be like the death of those who walk upright before Him, may He be blessed, without barrier, without impediment." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that his words were also spoken here in the Holy Spirit, and the intent behind them is ״תָּמוֹת נַפְשׁוֹ מוֹת״ — at the hand of ״יְשָׁרִים״, that Israel would kill him, as indeed came to pass." "Further, it may intend that upon reaching the day of death he would improve his ways from his evil deeds and his abominations, for he was a wicked man among the nations, and his she-ass, who served as his guardian, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Avodah Zarah 4b), testified about him. Rather, he longed that at the hour of death he would return and be upright like the upright of the nations. And since he recognized his temperament and saw that it was evil to the ultimate degree and was prevented from acting uprightly, therefore he asked for something that could possibly be — that at the hour of the distress of death he might be upright. And similar to this I have seen wicked men who told me explicitly that if they knew they would repent and immediately die, they would do so, but they know they cannot remain in repentance for long, because the word of the king is upon them — an old and foolish king (Ecclesiastes 4:13), may the Merciful One save us. And the wicked man did not ask to die the death of the righteous among Israel, for that is among the impossible — that he would attain to that level." "<b>וּתְהִי אַחֲרִיתִי כָּמֹהוּ.</b> He returns to the people; he said this regarding the World to Come, which is the end of the world. And you should know that HaShem causes to stand in judgment even for the righteous one who is worthy of that hidden felicity that is beyond the soul's grasp after death — therefore he prayed concerning both levels." "<b>Further,</b> according to what we explained regarding the intent of the phrase ״מוֹת יְשָׁרִים״ — that Israel would kill him — the phrase <b>וּתְהִי אַחֲרִיתִי</b> etc. is explained according to what has preceded us from the sayings of the men of truth: there is no kelipah in the world that does not contain within it a spark of holiness, the sod that animates it. And I have already elaborated on this matter in several places, and in parashat Shelach Lecha on the verse ״כִּי לַחְמֵנוּ הֵם״ etc. (Numbers 14:9). And that spark, when its bearer is killed by the hand of Israel, that good spark becomes purified, and sometimes it may cleave to him if he is clean without dross. And that is the meaning of <b>תָּמֹת נַפְשִׁי מוֹת יְשָׁרִים</b>; and through this my end shall be like his, for the spark cleaves to a man of Israel and behold, it is like him. And we find in the words of the mekubalim (Sefer HaGilgulim of the Arizal) that the soul of Balaam came into the she-ass of Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair, and this indicates that he was rectified in his end. And although he died by the hand of Israel, the spark was abominable and needed to be refined through gilgulim and purifications, and it endured until it came to the she-ass of Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair, and from there it ascended to its original place."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {8 "The phrase מֶלֶךְ זָקֵן וּכְסִיל ('an old and foolish king') is an allusion to Ecclesiastes 4:13, which the Or HaChaim applies here to the evil inclination; the parenthetical reference is supplied for clarity though he does not cite the verse explicitly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:32.276824152Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}